\begin{csabstract}
We study who profits from speed in Polymarket's in-play tennis moneylines, using public trade tapes for
<< V('univ.matches') >> matches. A walk-forward fast tier of wallets trading within
3\,s of a score move earns << V('ft.c.is') >>¢ per share after fees in sample and << V('ft.c.oos') >>¢ out of sample
(<< V('ft.months.is') >> and << V('ft.months.oos') >> months positive); other takers lose, and copying its trades 3\,s
later loses. At the fast tier's own fills a frozen book earns << V('v2.oos.c') >>¢ per share out of sample
(Sharpe << V('v2.oos.sr') >>) but turns negative when fees double. Simulated at a 1\,s licensed-feed baseline
(<< CV_LABEL >>), a computer-vision (CV) trader earns << V('cv.cal.oos.usd') >> a day out of sample under a calibrated,
post hoc stamp lag and << V('cv.pre.oos.usd') >> under the pre-registered one, which breaks even at
<< V('cv.pre.be.range') >>\,s of feed delay. Real money: none.

\vspace{2pt}\noindent\textbf{JEL:} G14, G12, C58.\enspace \textbf{Keywords:} prediction markets; latency arbitrage;
in-play betting; tennis; computer vision; market microstructure.
\end{csabstract}

% ======================================================================================== 1 Summary
\section{Summary}\label{sec:summary}
In-play tennis prediction markets are priced by whoever learns the point first. We measure who that is, what they earn
and what each second of speed is worth; our CV calls the point before the ball lands, and we price the feed it needs.
\begin{keybox}
\item \textbf{Strategy.} Call the point with CV before the ball lands; take the stale quote inside the
<< V('venue.delay') >>\,s taker delay. CV results: << CV_LABEL >>.
\item \textbf{Edge.} The book reprices << V('lat.book_vs_stamp') >>\,s \emph{before} the official stamp. The fast tier
earns << V('ft.c.is') >>\,/\,<< V('ft.c.oos') >>¢ per share after fees (IS\,/\,OOS) every month; other takers lose
<< V('oth.c.is') >>\,/\,<< V('oth.c.oos') >>¢.
\item \textbf{Headline OOS.} At fast-tier fills: << V('v2.oos.c') >>¢ << V('v2.oos.ci') >>, Sharpe
<< V('v2.oos.sr') >>. At the 1\,s baseline: << V('cv.cal.oos.usd') >>/day (Sharpe << V('cv.cal.oos.sr') >>; calibrated,
post hoc) or << V('cv.pre.oos.usd') >>/day (Sharpe << V('cv.pre.oos.sr') >>; pre-registered); break-even delay
<< V('cv.cal.be.range') >> or << V('cv.pre.be.range') >>\,s, so public streams and score feeds lose.
\item \textbf{What failed.} Fees \(\times\)2 OOS (<< V('v2.oos.fx2.c') >>¢); every blind test of a tradable book; the
live-book replay; table tennis; OOS after a central feed licence (<< V('fin.v2.oos.net_central') >>/day). Real money: none.
\end{keybox}

% ======================================================================================== 2 Hypothesis
\section{Economic hypothesis}\label{sec:hyp}
Win probability is a known function of the score. The leverage of point \(k\),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lev}
  \Delta(k) = \Pr(\text{win}\mid \text{point } k \text{ won}) - \Pr(\text{win}\mid \text{point } k \text{ lost}),
\end{equation}
follows from the exact point-level Markov chain \citep{klaassen2001}, with mean \(|\Delta(k)|\) of << V('markov.lev') >>
over the << V('markov.points') >> points of a simulated ATP best-of-three. Each point moves fair value by a known amount
at a known instant, and the news reaches traders in tiers (Fig.~\ref{fig:ladder}a): in-venue tracking; the official
stamp, an unmeasured << V('lat.stamp_band') >>\,s later; licensed video, << V('lat.video_band') >>\,s (vendor-stated);
public streams, \(\geq\)<< V('lat.webrtc_public') >>\,s (preliminary); score feeds, << V('lat.espn') >>--<< V('lat.wta') >>\,s behind
the book, which reprices a median << V('lat.book_vs_stamp') >>\,s \emph{before} the stamp (\(n = << V('lat.n_points') >>\)).
\begin{hypbox}
\textbf{H6.} \emph{Universe:} Polymarket ATP/WTA/Challenger moneylines. \emph{Behaviour:} wallets trading 0--3\,s
after a score move. \emph{Horizon:} 30\,s markout. \emph{Counterparty:} slower takers on an older tier.
\emph{Persistence:} physics, data licensing, the 1\,s taker delay. \emph{Prediction:} fast wallets chosen on past months
keep a positive net markout each month and out of sample. \emph{Falsifier:} \(\leq 0\) in over a third of months, or
out of sample. \emph{Trail:} H1--H4 committed before any result (\texttt{<< V('prereg.hyp.commit') >>},
<< V('prereg.hyp.time') >>); H6 written in sample and frozen before the OOS opened; v2 in
\texttt{HYPOTHESIS\_V2.md}; changes in \texttt{DEVIATIONS.md}.
\end{hypbox}
The edge is a structural constraint, not a risk premium, and tracks the structure: v2 earns << V('v2.reg.3s0') >>,
<< V('v2.reg.3s3') >>, << V('v2.reg.1s3') >> and << V('v2.reg.1s5') >>¢ per share in the 3\,s/0\%, 3\,s/3\%,
1\,s/3\% and 1\,s/5\% delay/fee regimes. The fast tier grew from << V('ft.wallets.first') >> to << V('ft.wallets.last') >>
wallets; its edge shrank << V('ft.slope') >>¢ a month (\(t = << V('ft.slope.t') >>\)) but stayed positive. Latency races
\citep{budish2015,aquilina2022} and in-play mispricing \citep{croxson2014,brown2012,brown2014} are known; new here: a
wallet-level map of the latency tier, a timing of every public source, a price on each second.

